  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Spectral networks — We introduce new geometric objects called spectral networks. Spectral net- works are networks of trajectories on Riemann surfaces obeying certain local rules.\n\n**Spectral networks**\n\n*Davide Gaiotto,1 Gregory W. Moore2 and Andrew Neitzke3*\n\n87 pp, 62 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We introduce new geometric objects called spectral networks. Spectral net- works are networks of trajectories on Riemann surfaces obeying certain local rules. Spec- tral networks arise naturally in four-dimensional N = 2 theories coupled to surface defects, particularly the theories of class S. In these theories spectral networks provide a useful tool for the computation of BPS degeneracies: the network directly determines the degen- eracies of solitons living on the surface defect, which in turn determine the degeneracies for particles living in the 4d bulk. Spectral networks also lead to a new map between ﬂat GL(K, C) connections on a two-dimensional surface C and ﬂat abelian connections on an appropriate branched cover Σ of C. This construction produces natural coordinate sys- tems on moduli spaces of ﬂat GL(K, C) connections on C, which we conjecture are cluster coordinate systems.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction and summary; 2 A brief review of theories of class S; 3 Geometric description of BPS states; 3.1 4d BPS states; 3.2 Solitons; 3.3 Framed 2d-4d BPS states; 3.4 Enhanced degeneracies; 3.5 A problem of signs; 4 Basics of framed 2d-4d indices; 4.1 Generating functions of framed 2d-4d indic; 4.2 Formal products and composition; 4.3 The spectral network W; 5 The spectral network W at fixed; 5.1 Walls as trajectories; 5.2 Joints and wall-crossing for; 5.3 The mass filtration; W[] and [] for small; 5.4 W[] and [] for general",
      "lang": "en",
